section.para:
The pipeline below uses model-free extractors and a synthetic ECB-style rate table. The dateline becomes the document date; both money spans keep their original offsets while the conversion layer restates them in USD. NumericExtractionExampleTest asserts the behavior shown here.
String text = "Berlin, 14 July 2026. The seller asked for €2.5 million, " + "but the buyer paid $2,400,000 after a week of talks.";
FxRates rates = EcbFxRates.load(ratesCsvStream); // e.g. 2026-07-13, 1.2000 USD per EUR
Document document = DocumentAnalyzer.builder() .add(new TemporalAnnotator(new CursorTemporalExtractor())) .add(new DocumentDateAnnotator()) .add(new MoneyAnnotator(new CursorMoneyExtractor())) .add(new MoneyConversionAnnotator(rates, "USD")) .build() .analyze(text);
// TEMPORALS / DOCUMENT_DATE: 14 July 2026 -> 2026-07-14 // MONEY: €2.5 million -> 2500000 EUR; $2,400,000 -> 2400000 USD // CONVERTED_MONEY: 3000000 USD and 2400000 USD on the same spans

			This example uses synthetic rates: 1 EUR equals 1.25 USD or 150 JPY.
			The weekend conversion uses the July 10 reference date and retains the span
			of the original amount.

String csv = "Date,USD,JPY,\n2026-07-10,1.25,150,\n";
FxRates rates = EcbFxRates.load(
    new ByteArrayInputStream(csv.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8)));

String text = "The fare was $10.";
MoneyAmount source = new CursorMoneyExtractor().extract(text).getFirst();
MoneyAmount converted = rates.convert(source, "JPY", LocalDate.of(2026, 7, 12))
    .orElseThrow();

// converted: 1200 JPY, with the original "$10" span
// July 17: available; July 18: empty (the quote is 8 days old)

			Use convert to convert an amount without multiplying by a rounded
			cross rate. With the synthetic quotes below, the rate is a repeating decimal,
			but the converted amount is exact.

String csv = "Date,USD,JPY\n2026-07-14,3,1\n";
FxRates rates = EcbFxRates.load(
    new ByteArrayInputStream(csv.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8)));
String text = "The fee was USD 3.";
MoneyAmount source = new CursorMoneyExtractor().extract(text).getFirst();
LocalDate date = LocalDate.of(2026, 7, 14);

BigDecimal rate = rates.rate("USD", "JPY", date).orElseThrow();
MoneyAmount converted = rates.convert(source, "JPY", date).orElseThrow();

// rate: 0.3333333333333333
// converted: 1 JPY, retaining the original "USD 3" span
// source.amount().multiply(rate): 0.9999999999999999

			In a document pipeline, conversions with no available rate are omitted from
			the converted layer. The source amount layer is unchanged. Match converted
			mentions to their source spans, not by list index. This example has no CHF
			quote, so the converted layer contains only the EUR and JPY mentions.

String csv = "Date,USD,JPY,\n2026-07-10,1.25,150,\n";
FxRates rates = EcbFxRates.load(
    new ByteArrayInputStream(csv.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8)));
String text = "2026-07-12: paid EUR 10, CHF 5, and JPY 150.";

Document document = DocumentAnalyzer.builder()
    .add(new TemporalAnnotator(new CursorTemporalExtractor()))
    .add(new DocumentDateAnnotator())
    .add(new MoneyAnnotator(new CursorMoneyExtractor()))
    .add(new MoneyConversionAnnotator(rates, "USD"))
    .build()
    .analyze(text);

List<Annotation<MoneyAmount>> converted =
    document.get(MoneyConversionAnnotator.CONVERTED_MONEY);

// EUR 10 -> 12.5 USD; JPY 150 -> 1.25 USD
// CHF 5 remains in MONEY but has no conversion.

section.programlisting:
String csv = "Date,USD\n2026-07-14,1.25\n";
FxRates rates = EcbFxRates.load(
    new ByteArrayInputStream(csv.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8)));
List<DocumentAnnotator> annotators = NumericPacks.annotators(Locale.GERMANY);
annotators.add(new MoneyConversionAnnotator(rates, "USD"));

DocumentAnalyzer.Builder builder = DocumentAnalyzer.builder();
annotators.forEach(builder::add);
Document document = builder.build()
    .analyze("2026-07-14: paid EUR 10 for 3 kg yesterday.");

// MONEY: 10 EUR; CONVERTED_MONEY: 12.5 USD, both on the "EUR 10" span
// QUANTITIES: 3 kg; yesterday: 2026-07-13; DOCUMENT_DATE: 2026-07-14

section.para:
			CursorTemporalExtractor reports the date part of a timestamp at
			day granularity. The span excludes the time and offset. The written calendar
			date is used without conversion to UTC, including for relative-date resolution.

String text = "Received 2026-07-14T23:30:00.125-04:00. Reviewed yesterday.";
Document document = NumericPacks.temporal().analyze(text);

// TEMPORALS: 2026-07-14 -> 2026-07-14; yesterday -> 2026-07-13
// DOCUMENT_DATE: 2026-07-14, on the date-only span [9, 19)

			By default, TemporalAnnotator extracts absolute dates and uses
			the first absolute day mention as the reference for another scan. This resolves
			expressions such as yesterday, 3 days ago, and
			next quarter. Relative expressions retain their origin and do not
			replace the document date.

Document document = NumericPacks.temporal()
    .analyze("Berlin, 14 July 2026. The buyer paid yesterday.");

// TEMPORALS: 14 July 2026 -> 2026-07-14; yesterday -> 2026-07-13
// DOCUMENT_DATE: 2026-07-14, on the span of the dateline

			Without an absolute day mention or a configured reference date, relative
			expressions are omitted. Resolution does not depend on the system clock.

Document document = NumericPacks.temporal().analyze("The buyer paid yesterday.");

// TEMPORALS: empty

			To use a date from metadata, pass it to
			TemporalAnnotator(TemporalExtractor, LocalDate) or
			NumericPacks.temporal(LocalDate). This reference takes precedence
			when resolving relative expressions. It is not added to the document-date
			layer, which requires an absolute day mention with a text span.

Document document = NumericPacks.temporal(LocalDate.of(2021, 1, 1))
    .analyze("Filed yesterday. Review this week; follow-up next month.");

// TEMPORALS: yesterday -> 2020-12-31; this week -> 2020-W53; next month -> 2021-02
// DOCUMENT_DATE: empty, the reference came from metadata

			Temporal values must have the same spans as their annotations. The selected
			value must be a valid calendar date; an invalid first date is not replaced by
			a later date. The source layer is unchanged. This example builds a layer in
			reverse order to show selection by text position.

String text = "Filed 2026-07-14. Due 2026-08-01.";
List<Annotation<TemporalExpression>> mentions = new CursorTemporalExtractor().extract(text)
    .reversed().stream().map(value -> new Annotation<>(value.span(), value)).toList();

Document input = Document.of(text).with(TemporalAnnotator.TEMPORALS, mentions);
Document document = new DocumentDateAnnotator().annotate(input);
Annotation<LocalDate> date = document.get(DocumentDateAnnotator.DOCUMENT_DATE).getFirst();

// TEMPORALS retains list order: 2026-08-01, 2026-07-14
// DOCUMENT_DATE: 2026-07-14, with the original span [6, 16)

			CursorMoneyExtractor.forRegion(Locale) and the regional packs derive
			the notation from the locale's own DecimalFormatSymbols, not from a
			hard-coded country list, and resolve ambiguous currency symbols in the same step.

String text = "Der Kaufer zahlte 2.400.000 EUR fur 1.250,5 GB Speicher.";

Document german = NumericPacks.fullPipeline(Locale.GERMANY).analyze(text);
// MONEY: 2.400.000 EUR -> 2400000 EUR
// QUANTITIES: 1.250,5 GB -> 1250.5 GB

Document american = NumericPacks.fullPipeline().analyze(text);
// MONEY and QUANTITIES: empty, the text is not written in that notation

			An amount may name its currency in words instead of a symbol or an ISO code:
			50 euros, 1.2 million dollars,
			3 billion pounds. The word follows the amount and any scale marker,
			and the mention covers the whole phrase.

List<MoneyAmount> mentions = new CursorMoneyExtractor()
    .extract("the fund raised 1.2 million dollars and 50 euros");

// 1.2 million dollars -> 1200000 USD
// 50 euros -> 50 EUR

Unit matching is case-sensitive. The built-in set excludes ambiguous words such as in. A set passed to CursorQuantityExtractor(Set) replaces the built-in units; percentage recognition is independent of that set. Custom tokens contain letters only and are rejected at construction time if they are empty or exceed six UTF-16 code units. A supplementary Unicode letter occupies 2 code units. A letter, number, combining mark, or identifier connector following a unit prevents a partial match. For example, 5m² is not reported as 5 meters; area units are not supported. Combining marks on preceding text are classified with the base character, so a quantity after an emoji remains valid.
Document document = DocumentAnalyzer.builder() .add(new QuantityAnnotator(new CursorQuantityExtractor())) .build() .analyze("battery lasts 12 hr at 45% load");
// QUANTITIES: 12 hr -> 12 hr; 45% -> 45 %

			This custom set recognizes kilograms and percentages, but excludes the
			built-in meter unit.

List<Quantity> mentions = new CursorQuantityExtractor(Set.of("kg"))
    .extract("Load 5kg across 2m at 45% capacity.");

// QUANTITIES: 5kg -> 5 kg; 45% -> 45 %
// 2m is not included because m is not in the custom set.

			Recognized currency amounts take precedence over measurements, including
			custom units. The quantity extractor uses the selected number notation and
			the default CursorMoneyExtractor currency-symbol table. This
			avoids interpreting the scale suffix in $3m as meters, while
			preserving separate measurements and their original offsets.

String text = "Paid $3m for 3m of pipe and 5kg of fittings; USD 2m remained.";
Document document = NumericPacks.fullPipeline().analyze(text);

// MONEY: $3m -> 3000000 USD; USD 2m -> 2000000 USD
// QUANTITIES: 3m -> 3 m; 5kg -> 5 kg
